---
import BaseLayout from '@shared/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AdSense from '@shared/components/AdSense.astro';

const title = 'Free Financial Calculators for US Home Buyers & Investors';
const description = 'Free, fast financial calculators for mortgage payments, auto loans, compound interest, paychecks, and 2026 US federal income tax.';

const calculators = [
  { href: '/mortgage-calculator/',         name: 'Mortgage Calculator',          blurb: 'Estimate your monthly mortgage payment including principal, interest, property tax, and home insurance.' },
  { href: '/auto-loan-calculator/',        name: 'Auto Loan Calculator',         blurb: 'Compute your monthly car payment, total interest, and out-the-door cost. Sales tax included.' },
  { href: '/compound-interest-calculator/',name: 'Compound Interest Calculator', blurb: 'Project your investment growth over time with regular contributions. Year-by-year chart.' },
  { href: '/paycheck-calculator/',         name: 'Paycheck Calculator',          blurb: 'See your real take-home pay after federal tax, FICA, state tax, and 401(k).' },
  { href: '/income-tax-calculator/',       name: 'Income Tax Calculator (2026)', blurb: 'Estimate your 2026 US federal income tax with full bracket breakdown.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} pathname="/" schemaType="WebSite">
  <section class="mb-10 max-w-4xl space-y-4">
    <p class="kdp-section-label">Transparent math for everyday money decisions</p>
    <h1 class="text-5xl font-semibold leading-[1.02] sm:text-6xl">Free Financial Calculators</h1>
    <p class="max-w-3xl text-xl leading-8 text-stone-700">
      Fast, no-signup financial calculators for US users. Estimate payments, taxes, take-home pay, and long-term growth with transparent step-by-step math.
    </p>
    <p class="max-w-3xl text-stone-600 leading-7">
      CalcKDP focuses on decisions where the formula matters: comparing a home payment against your budget, checking the real cost of a car loan, estimating paycheck deductions, and seeing how contributions change investment growth over time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <AdSense adSlot="home-top" style="min-height:90px" />

  <section class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mt-8">
    {calculators.map((c) => (
      <a href={c.href} class="kdp-card block rounded-2xl p-5 focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-teal-700 focus-visible:ring-offset-2">
        <article>
          <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold tracking-normal mb-2">{c.name}</h2>
          <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-stone-600 mb-4">{c.blurb}</p>
          <p class="kdp-link text-sm">Use Calculator -></p>
        </article>
      </a>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12 grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
    <div class="kdp-prose space-y-3">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-normal text-stone-950">What you can calculate here</h2>
      <p>
        Each calculator is built around a specific financial question. Mortgage and auto loan tools show monthly payment and total interest. Paycheck and income tax tools separate federal tax, FICA, state tax, deductions, and estimated take-home pay. The compound interest calculator shows how starting balance, contributions, rate, and time work together.
      </p>
      <p>
        Results are estimates, not financial advice. We show formulas, assumptions, and limitations so you can spot when a calculator is a useful planning shortcut and when you should verify details with a lender, payroll provider, tax professional, or official IRS source.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="kdp-prose space-y-3">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-normal text-stone-950">How the calculators are different</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Inputs stay in your browser and update instantly as you type.</li>
        <li>Calculator pages include a visible “Show the math” section with the actual formula and your current inputs.</li>
        <li>Tax-related pages name the filing year and bracket source when a published table is used.</li>
        <li>Related calculators link to each other so you can move from monthly payment to total tax or take-home pay without hunting through menus.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="kdp-prose mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-4">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-normal text-stone-950">A short planning workflow</h2>
    <p>
      Most personal-finance questions on this site are really a sequence, not a single form. Start with cash you might actually bank, then price the monthly obligation, then ask what leftover cash does if it is invested. A typical path:
    </p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
      <li>
        Estimate take-home pay on the <a href="/paycheck-calculator/">paycheck calculator</a> (W-2, tax year 2026). If you only need the federal income-tax slice, use the <a href="/income-tax-calculator/">income tax calculator</a> instead.
      </li>
      <li>
        Price the housing or car draft on the <a href="/mortgage-calculator/">mortgage</a> or <a href="/auto-loan-calculator/">auto loan</a> calculator. Compare that payment with the take-home number — not with gross salary.
      </li>
      <li>
        If the question is “pay extra on the loan versus invest,” run the same leftover dollars through the <a href="/compound-interest-calculator/">compound interest calculator</a>. Treat the rate as a planning assumption, not a forecast.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Longer explainers that walk the same formulas by hand live on the portal:
      <a href="https://kdp.cool/guides/">kdp.cool/guides/</a>.
      Start with
      <a href="https://kdp.cool/guides/how-to-read-take-home-pay/">how to read take-home pay</a>,
      <a href="https://kdp.cool/guides/how-monthly-mortgage-payments-are-calculated/">how monthly mortgage payments are calculated</a>,
      or
      <a href="https://kdp.cool/guides/how-us-federal-income-tax-brackets-work/">how US federal income tax brackets work</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="kdp-prose mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-4">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-semibold tracking-normal text-stone-950">When not to rely only on an online calculator</h2>
    <p>
      These tools are educational estimates. They are useful for comparing inputs and learning the formula. They are the wrong last step when a real counterparty will use a richer rule set than a browser form.
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
      <li>A lender’s Loan Estimate can include PMI, HOA, flood coverage, points, and fees this mortgage form does not collect.</li>
      <li>A dealer worksheet can include title, documentation, add-ons, and a state sales-tax basis (especially trade-in credits) that the auto-loan page intentionally simplifies.</li>
      <li>Payroll follows IRS Publication 15-T and your W-4. Annual tax ÷ 26 is a planning model, not Friday’s stub.</li>
      <li>A filed Form 1040 can include credits, capital gains, and state tax that the federal income-tax page does not compute. Brackets here are tax year 2026 from IRS Rev. Proc. 2025-32.</li>
      <li>Investment projections here are nominal and pre-tax. Inflation, fees, and sequence of returns can dominate a 30-year 7% illustration.</li>
      <li>1099 / self-employment income is out of scope on the paycheck tool. Self-employment tax is not “the same FICA line twice” in this UI.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      When a calculator and an official document disagree, the official document wins. Use CalcKDP to understand the shape of the number; use the lender, payroll provider, IRS, SSA, or a licensed professional for the number you sign.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-500">Last reviewed 2026-09-16. Results are educational estimates, not financial, tax, or legal advice.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-[#e6d9c8]">
    <h2 class="kdp-section-label mb-3">About this site</h2>
    <ul class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-3 text-sm">
      <li><a href="https://kdp.cool/guides/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Planning guides</a></li>
      <li><a href="/about/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">About CalcKDP</a></li>
      <li><a href="/privacy/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Privacy Policy</a></li>
      <li><a href="/terms/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Terms of Service</a></li>
      <li><a href="/contact/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Contact</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <AdSense adSlot="home-bottom" style="min-height:90px" />
</BaseLayout>
